We have,

\left[1+\left(\frac{d y}{d x}\right)^{5}\right]^{1 / 3}=\frac{d^{2} y}{d x^{2}} \Rightarrow\left(\frac{d^{2} y}{d x^{2}}\right)^{3}=1+\left(\frac{d y}{d x}\right)^{5}

$\therefore$ Order $=2$ and degree $=3$

We have,

\begin{gathered}
y \frac{d y}{d x}+x=c \\
\Rightarrow \quad y \frac{d y}{d x}=(c-x) \\
\Rightarrow \quad y d y=(c-x) d x
\end{gathered}

\begin{array}{ll}
\Rightarrow & y d y=-(x-c) d x \\
\Rightarrow & \int y d y=-\int(x-c) d x \\
\Rightarrow \quad & \frac{y^{2}}{2}=-\frac{(x-c)^{2}}{2}+K \\
\Rightarrow & (x-c)^{2}+y^{2}=2 K
\end{array}

which is a circle with centre $(c, 0)$ on the $X$-axis.

Given, equation of parabola is $y^{2}=4 a x \quad \text{...(i)}$
Differentiating $\mathrm{E} \mathrm{g}$. (i) w.r.t. $x$, we get

\begin{aligned}
2 y \frac{d y}{d x} &=4 a \\
\Rightarrow \quad \frac{d y}{d x} &=\frac{2 a}{y} \Rightarrow a=\frac{y}{2} \frac{d y}{d x}
\end{aligned}

Substituting the value of $a$ in Eq. (i), we get

\begin{aligned}
y^{2} &=4 \cdot \frac{y}{2} \frac{d y}{d x} x \\
\Rightarrow \quad y^{2} &=2 x y \frac{d y}{d x} \Rightarrow \frac{d y}{d x}=\frac{y}{2 x}
\end{aligned}

Given, differential equation is

\frac{d y}{d x}+y \cdot g^{\prime}(x)=g(x) \cdot g^{\prime}(x)

This is in the form of linear differential equation.
So, $\quad \mathrm{IF}=e^{\int g^{\prime}(x) d x}=e^{g(x)}$
Required solution is

y \cdot e^{g(x)}=\int g(x) \cdot g^{\prime}(x) \cdot e^{g(x)} d x+c^{\prime}

Put $g(x)=t \Rightarrow g^{\prime}(x) d x=d t$
\therefore y \cdot e^{g(x)}=\int t e^{t} d t+c^{\prime}

\begin{aligned}
&\Rightarrow y \cdot e^{g(x)}=t e^{t}-e^{t}+c^{\prime} \\
&\Rightarrow y \cdot e^{g(x)}=(g(x)-1) e^{g(x)}+c^{\prime} \\
&\text { or }(y-g(x)+1) e^{g(x)}=c^{\prime}
\end{aligned}

Taking log on both sides, we get

g(x)+\log (1+y-g(x))=c

Where, $c=\log c^{\prime}$

Given, differential equation is

\begin{aligned}
&\left(2 x-10 y^{3}\right) \frac{d y}{d x}+y=0 \Rightarrow \frac{d y}{d x}=\frac{-y}{2 x-10 y^{3}} \\
\Rightarrow \quad & \frac{d x}{d y}=\frac{2 x-10 y^{3}}{-y}=\frac{-2 x}{y}+10 y^{2} \\
\Rightarrow \quad & \frac{d x}{d y}+\frac{2}{y} x=10 y^{2}
\end{aligned}

Compare with linear differential equation

\frac{d x}{d y}+P x=Q \Rightarrow \mathrm{IF}=e^{\int \frac{2}{y} d y}=e^{\log y^{2}}=y^{2}

$\therefore$ Required solution is $x \cdot y^{2}=\int 10 y^{2} \cdot y^{2} d y+C$ i.e.

x y^{2}=2 y^{5}+C

Given, differential equation is
$\frac{d y}{d x}-1=e^{x-y}\begin{array}{lrl}
\text { Put } & & x-y=t \\
\Rightarrow & 1-\frac{d y}{d x}=\frac{d t}{d x}
\end{array}$
So, given equation becomes,

\begin{aligned}
-\frac{d t}{d x} &=e^{t} \\
-e^{-t} d t &=d x
\end{aligned}

Integrating both sides, we get

\begin{aligned}
&e^{-t}=x+c \\
&e^{-(x-y)}=x+c
\end{aligned}
